            <ArticleTitle>Cervical &amp; thoracic manipulations: Acute effects upon pain pressure threshold and self-reported pain in experimentally induced shoulder pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>227-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.math.2015.08.009</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1356-689X(15)00171-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Emerging evidence suggests that cervical and thoracic joint manipulations may be advocated in treating patients with shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the acute effects of cervical, cervicothoracic, and thoracic joint manipulations on outcomes of self-reported pain and pain pressure threshold in experimentally induced shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Repeated measures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty (20) healthy volunteers were tested on two sessions. Session 1 consisted on baseline assessment of pain pressure threshold testing over the infraspinatus bilaterally and self-reported shoulder pain using the shoulder pain and disability index (SPADI) pain scale. An isokinetic exercise protocol was used to induce delayed onset muscle soreness. In session 2 (24-48 h later), all variables were reassessed before and immediately after a combination of cervical, cervicothoracic and thoracic manipulations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">SPADI pain scale scores were significantly different between time points (p &lt; 0.001): the exercise protocol significantly increased reported pain [mean increase 14.1, p &lt; 0.001] while the manipulation significantly decreased reported pain (mean decrease 5.60, p &lt; 0.001)) although pain remained higher than baseline levels. Pain pressure threshold differences were also found between time points (p = 0.001): manipulation significantly increased pain threshold bilaterally (p &lt; 0.001) similar to baseline levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Cervical, cervicothoracic, and thoracic joint manipulations acutely increased pain pressure threshold and decreased self-reported shoulder pain in participants with experimentally induced shoulder pain. Physiotherapists may consider the combination of such techniques to achieve short-term hypoalgesic effects and facilitate the application of more active interventions.</AbstractText>
